{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a 'critical preference' grounded in verisimilitude (truth-likeness), not probability. This avoids induction because it posits an objective, albeit unknown, distance from truth that severe testing approximates without assigning likelihood.","counterconditions":["If Popper's own later rejection of verisimilitude as a formalizable concept invalidates the metric used to define 'better'.","If the definition of 'severe test' implicitly relies on inductive background knowledge about what constitutes a risky prediction.","If the leap from 'survived tests' to 'closer to truth' cannot be made without assuming nature's uniformity."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between logical probability (which remains zero or undefined for universal laws) and verisimilitude (content of truth minus content of falsity). A theory that survives severe tests has demonstrated higher empirical content and resilience, suggesting it is closer to the truth. The preference is a rational bet on truth-likeness, justified by the logical structure of the tests themselves, not by an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past.","optional_refs":["5e9de594afba8ef14af435e0a77e52ba402ff559e198a1b582ee283761e8e8b8","c3d0d530922340326052900c8e60b03764676c17de3ed218b8873952ac3cdbaf"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back into Popper's system because any claim that a 'better-corroborated' theory is preferable for future action implicitly assumes that past performance predicts future success, which is the very definition of induction Popper sought to eliminate.","counterconditions":["If Popper can demonstrate a purely deductive link between surviving a test and being a better candidate for future tests without invoking temporal uniformity.","If the preference is redefined as purely arbitrary or aesthetic, devoid of predictive expectation.","If the concept of 'severity' can be defined entirely independently of any inductive background assumptions."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To prefer Theory A over Theory B because A survived tests that B failed (or A survived more severe tests) is to act on the expectation that A will continue to succeed where B might fail. This expectation is an inductive inference. Without the assumption that the future will resemble the past (uniformity of nature), the fact that A survived yesterday offers no logical reason to expect it to survive tomorrow. Thus, the 'pragmatic' preference is indistinguishable from inductive probabilistic reasoning in practice.","optional_refs":["c4a204b81be1fa46d76a38c4e46aff3301e98f0f61d7f49d8a2abba76c9feeb5","c69354a726c86c6a572c591e0e70c3805d37e296eb699aeeb840c57eca4a9897"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.88},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma dissolves if we view corroboration not as a measure of truth or probability, but as a report of the current state of critical discussion, making preference a procedural necessity rather than an epistemic inference.","counterconditions":["If scientists clearly act on the belief that corroborated theories are more likely to be true, not just procedurally convenient.","If the 'game' of science aims at truth, rendering purely procedural rules insufficient for goal attainment.","If the severity of a test cannot be assessed without inductive background knowledge."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Corroboration is purely historical: it reports 'Theory T has been tested and not yet refuted.' It makes no claim about T's future. The preference for T is not an epistemic judgment that T is better, but a procedural rule of the game of science: 'Do not discard a theory that has not been falsified in favor of one that has, or in favor of nothing.' This avoids induction because it makes no prediction; it simply dictates the current move in the dialectic.","optional_refs":["fcc20d26771443f9f9e8acd6740a395ed0cbc3e6f7b8704366af89a6eb510fee"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's defense holds only if we accept a dual-aspect theory of rationality: theoretical reason (logic) forbids induction, but practical reason (evolutionary epistemology) mandates preference for the fittest surviving hypothesis.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly denied any evolutionary basis for scientific rationality.","If the gap between 'survival value' and 'truth approximation' cannot be bridged without induction.","If this dual-aspect view renders the concept of 'objective knowledge' incoherent."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"This candidate posits that Popper implicitly shifts domains. In the domain of logic/proof, induction is dead. In the domain of action/biology, organisms (including scientists) must conjecture and select based on survival value. Corroboration is the scientific analog of evolutionary fitness. We prefer the corroborated theory not because logic demands it, but because our cognitive apparatus is evolved to seek patterns that survive.","optional_refs":["e799ae8dcbf57180d8a517149d5f62b6e410385deaed05923499182e028fec2e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible as a 'methodological fiction': scientists must act *as if* the better-corroborated theory is true to facilitate further testing, even while knowing logically that it may be false. This is a pragmatic stance required for the growth of knowledge, not an ontological claim.","counterconditions":["If this 'fiction' leads to dogmatism that prevents the consideration of radically different alternatives.","If the strategy fails to produce more rapid falsifications than random selection.","If Popper explicitly rejected instrumentalist or fictionalist interpretations of theory choice."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Science progresses by treating the best-tested theory as the 'working truth' temporarily. This is not induction (believing it is probably true) nor verisimilitude (knowing it is closer to truth), but a strategic decision to maximize the efficiency of falsification. If we treated all unfalsified theories as equal, research would stall. The preference is a tool for generating new problems, not a conclusion about the world.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The problem is a pseudo-problem created by conflating 'rationality' with 'certainty.' Popper's system allows for rational preference without certainty or probability; the cost is accepting that rationality is fallible and temporary, offering no guarantee of success.","counterconditions":["If 'responsibility' in choosing theories inevitably implies an expectation of success (induction).","If the lack of guarantee renders the preference arbitrary compared to other unfalsified theories.","If the scientific community requires more than 'temporary responsibility' to justify large-scale resource allocation."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Rationality in Popper's view is simply the commitment to critical scrutiny. Preferring the better-corroborated theory is rational because it is the one that has withstood the most scrutiny so far. It is not 'defensible' in the sense of being proven right, but in the sense of being the most responsible choice given the current critical situation. The 'smuggling' charge fails because it assumes rationality requires a foundation (induction or probability), which Popper denies.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}